{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1425705205/5/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"58f5dbd5\" lines=\"20-34\">\n<description>\nCarve color-matched motifs into solid blocks and pack them into a compact grid.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Several solid 5×5 monochrome blocks, each a distinct non-bg color.\n- For each block color, a small same-colored motif sprite elsewhere on the grid.\n\nRule:\n- Match each motif to its same-colored 5×5 block.\n- Center the motif within the block and recolor those cells to bg (punching holes).\n- Pack all carved blocks into an output grid preserving their original row/column order, separated by 1-cell gaps and a 1-cell outer border.\n\nKey insight: The motif acts as a stencil — it's not stamped onto the block but carved out of it as background holes.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"4a21e3da\" lines=\"8-20\">\n<description>\nCut a sprite along border-control rays and slide pieces to grid corners.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg, fg (main object, ~centered), ctrl (1-2 pixels on grid edges).\n\nRule:\n- Draw rays inward from each ctrl pixel along its row/col, painting only bg cells up to the farthest fg pixel (intermittent).\n- Rays partition the grid; fg on the cut line stays in place.\n- Cut pieces in regions touching a control edge slide to that corner; others vanish.\n\nKey insight: Only regions sharing a border edge with a control pixel keep their piece; the ray skips fg cells.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"88bcf3b4\" lines=\"229-241\">\n<description>\nSwing an irregular line segment around a nearby obstacle sprite, preserving its pixel count.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg, bar color (straight lines perpendicular to the grid edge, one end on the border), segment color (irregular stroke attached to bar's inner endpoint), obstacle color (sprite near the bar terminal).\n\nRule:\n- Each bar is perpendicular to the edge it touches; its inner (terminal) endpoint has an attached irregular segment.\n- The segment detaches and re-routes: it travels from the terminal toward the nearest obstacle, hugs the obstacle's boundary, then exits at a 45° diagonal.\n- Total painted pixels equal the original segment length.\n\nKey insight: The segment behaves like a rope swung around the obstacle — attach at the nearest point, trace the skin, exit diagonally, conserving length.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"3e6067c3\" lines=\"7-20\">\n<description>\nConnect framed squares via colored bridges following a control-pixel sequence.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Border color forms equal-sized framed squares with unique inner fill colors.\n- Control row below squares: colored pixels read left-to-right encode visitation order.\n\nRule:\n- Start at the square matching first control color c0.\n- For each consecutive pair (ci, ci+1), find the ci+1-colored square aligned in the same row or column band, preferring nearest.\n- Draw a bridge colored ci between the pair, spanning the gap with inner-square thickness.\n\nKey insight: Row/column-band alignment plus proximity resolves ambiguity when inner colors repeat.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"269e22fb\" lines=\"46-60\">\n<description>\nComplete a visible crop into the shared 20x20 binary motif in its matching orientation.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid is a two-color rectangular crop of one rotation/reflection of the same 20x20 motif.\n- One color is the motif foreground and the other color is the motif background.\n\nRule:\n- Try each input color as the foreground mask.\n- Match that mask against every possible window in every dihedral orientation of the motif.\n- Repaint the uniquely matched full 20x20 motif with the matched foreground/background colors.\n\nKey insight:\n- The examples are partial windows of one canonical binary pattern; the local crop is distinctive enough to identify the full orientation and offset.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"a25697e4\" lines=\"9-23\">\n<description>\nDock movable tools into border-anchored frames by filling cavities.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Border-touching components cluster into anchor frames with interior bg cavities.\n- Non-border 2-color components are movable tools.\n\nRule:\n- Match each tool to a frame whose cavity size equals one of its color-region counts.\n- Rotate/flip the tool so one color fills the cavity exactly; the other stays outside.\n- If the docking orientation uses a reflection, swap the tool's two colors.\n\nKey insight: Cavity size determines tool–frame pairing; reflection triggers color swap.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"6e453dd6\" lines=\"39-52\">\n<description>\nSlide black shapes rightward to a grey separator, then shoot red rays from enclosed holes.\n\nInput structure:\n- A vertical grey (5) separator column divides the grid into left and right halves.\n- Black shapes sit on the left side against a background color (bg).\n\nRule:\n- Translate each black connected component rightward until its right edge is flush with the separator.\n- After sliding, find enclosed bg regions (holes) within the black shapes that touch the column just left of the separator.\n- For each row spanned by such a hole, emit a red ray across the entire right half of the grid.\n\nKey insight: Only holes adjacent to the separator trigger rays — the sliding creates enclosures that act as \"nozzles\" projecting rightward.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"409aa875\" lines=\"68-81\">\n<description>\nFire projectiles from 3-pixel triangle sprites; mark collisions with maroon or blue.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Non-bg 3-pixel sprites shaped as axis-aligned L's or diagonal V's.\n\nRule:\n- Each triangle has an apex and an emission direction determined by its shape: L-shapes fire diagonally away from the missing corner; V-shapes fire along the axis away from the open side.\n- A projectile lands exactly 5 cells from the apex in the emission direction.\n- If it hits an existing sprite, that entire sprite is recolored to maroon (9).\n- If two projectiles land on the same empty cell, that cell becomes blue (1); a lone projectile on empty bg paints maroon (9).\n\nKey insight: The triangle's orientation encodes both direction and target; dual-projectile collisions on open bg produce blue, not maroon.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"71e489b6\" lines=\"7-19\">\n<description>\nHighlight black defects in blue foreground by surrounding them with orange.\n\nInput structure:\n- Blue (1) foreground on black (0) bg; small black holes or protrusions within blue regions; occasional thin blue spurs into black areas.\n\nRule:\n- Thin blue pixels mostly surrounded by black are absorbed into the background.\n- Small black components (≤3 cells) and leaf pixels of larger black components are \"defects.\"\n- Each defect gets an orange (7) 8-neighbor halo; the defect pixels themselves stay black.\n\nKey insight: Only structural anomalies—tiny isolated patches and dangling endpoints—are marked, not bulk black regions.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"e12f9a14\" lines=\"138-152\">\n<description>\nEmit diagonal beams from framed 2x2 blocks through frame gaps; beams interact on collision.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Wall color (e.g. green) forms 4x4 frames around 2x2 monochrome blocks.\n- Each frame has one or more gap pixels where the wall is missing.\n\nRule:\n- Each gap emits a beam of the inner block's color, traveling diagonally outward (direction set by gap position relative to the block).\n- Beams paint every cell they cross until they hit the grid edge or collide.\n- 90° collision: both beams turn 45° toward their bisector direction; the intersection cell is left blank (1-pixel gap).\n- 45° collision: beams merge into a zigzag (alternating cardinal + diagonal steps) with no gap at the meeting point.\n\nKey insight: Two distinct collision rules — 90° produces a gap and single-pixel diagonal lines; 45° produces no gap and thicker zigzag bands.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"a6f40cea\" lines=\"74-88\">\n<description>\nComplete partial paths inside a framed rectangle by projecting external color traces inward.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg, a 1px rectangular frame (border color) with uniform interior fill.\n- Colored components outside the frame touching its edges.\n\nRule:\n- Output = the frame's interior, filled with the fill color.\n- Map each outside component's frame-touch points into interior coordinates.\n- Monochrome components become solid Manhattan stripes between mapped endpoints.\n- Two-color components become alternating-color Manhattan paths.\n\nKey insight: Endpoint colors of two-color traces anchor the alternating phase deterministically.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"db0c5428\" lines=\"32-51\">\n<description>\nExpand a 3x3 block motif into a 5x5 block motif around its missing center.\n\nInput structure:\n- Teal background surrounds one 9x9 foreground motif.\n- The motif is a 3x3 array of 3x3 blocks; the middle block is empty teal.\n- Colored pixels immediately around the empty middle block encode the border\n  of the missing center block.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the original 9x9 motif in place.\n- Copy each of the eight surrounding 3x3 blocks to the opposite outer slot\n  of the implied 5x5 block grid, without rotating it.\n- Fill the center block from the eight pixels around the hole; its center is\n  the motif's majority foreground color.\n\nKey insight: The visible 9x9 motif is the middle of a larger periodic\nblock-level pattern, and the empty center is reconstructed from its immediate\ninner boundary.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
